rom ep-exdos13.rom page 0 cpu z80
default observed

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Function 29 (the function table's $058A): a fresh FAT and root directory on a drive

@05A6 FN29_INIT_FS
  EXDOS function 29 (table word at $058A): prepares drive C, has its unit's driver run request 8,
  then writes a fresh file system: FAT entries 0 and 1, every other FAT entry 0, and an
  emptied root directory.
  1. $0608 selects the drive (B = 2) and drops its buffers; DE is handed on to it.
  2. Driver request 8 through $0470, with C = the caller's page-1 segment (IY+$2D); an answer
     of $BD becomes error $AF (Invalid drive).
  3. INIT_FS_FAT writes the FAT and clears the directory (function 28 joins there too).
  Entry: C = the drive number; DE = handed to $0608; IY = EXDOS's base.
  Exit: A = 0, Z.
  Error: NZ, A = the error code (from $0608, the driver or the drive select).
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, IX, all alternates; page 1.
  Keeps: IY.
  .05A6 Select drive C (B = 2) and drop its buffers
  .05A9 No such drive or no disk?
  .05AB Request 8 to the unit's driver
  .05AD C = the caller's page-1 segment, saved by $0505
  .05B4 Request done?
  .05B6 Answer $BD?
  .05B9 Then error $AF (Invalid drive)

@05BC INIT_FS_FAT
  Writes a fresh FAT for drive C and clears its root directory: FAT[0] = $F00 + the media byte
  (record+$1D), FAT[1] = $FFF, then FAT[2] up to the last cluster 0 (INIT_FS_CLEAR). Function 28
  ($05A4) joins here too.
  .05BC HL = the drive's record (paged into page 1)
  .05BF Drive selected?
  .05C1 Keep the record twice: the second slot gets the media byte
  .05C7 C = the media byte at record+$1D
  .05C8 BC = $0F00 + media byte, the value of FAT entry 0
  .05CA H = media byte, kept on the stack for the end
  .05CC DE = 0: cluster 0
  .05CD FAT[0] = $F00 + media byte
  .05D0 Cluster 1
  .05D1 B >= $10 writes $FFF
  .05D4 FAT[1] = $FFF

@05D7 INIT_FS_CLEAR
  Sets every FAT entry from cluster 2 on to 0 (free) up to the last cluster (record+$16 holds
  the cluster count + 2), then clears the root directory's sectors (record+$12 up to
  record+$14 - 1) through DIR_CLEAR_SECS when the media byte is below $FE, and writes the
  drive's changed buffers back ($0BAD).
  .05D7 Next cluster
  .05DB FAT[DE] = 0: free
  .05DF HL = the word at record+$16, the cluster limit
  .05E7 The last cluster is limit - 1
  .05E8 Was that the last one?
  .05ED Record+$12: the root directory's first sector
  .05F5 DE = record+$14: the sector after it (the data area)
  .05FA L = the directory's sector count
  .05FC A = the media byte saved at $05CB
  .05FD Below $FE?
  .0601 DE = the directory's last sector
  .0602 Clear the directory, last sector first
  .0605 Write back the drive's changed buffers

@0CBE SEL_DRIVE_REC
  Selects drive C for a call that wants its record (B = 0): IX = IY+$06A1, then the code from
  $0CC9 (shared with $0608, which enters with B = 2) finds the drive's unit through $02D1,
  stores it at IY+$2B, pages the unit's segment into page 1 and checks the disk in it.
  Entry: C = the drive number (0 = the default drive, 1-26 = A-Z); IY = EXDOS's base.
  Exit: A = 0, HL = the drive's record (in page 1).
  Error: A = $AF (Invalid drive) with Z set when the drive has no unit; callers test A.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, IX, all alternates; page 1.
  Keeps: IY.
  .0CBE B = 0: the caller wants the record
  .0CC0 IX = IY+$06A1, a work block

@1193 FAT_SET_ENTRY
  Writes the 12-bit FAT entry of cluster DE: B >= $10 writes $FFF (end of chain), else BC.
  The code from $119C finds the entry (FAT_ENTRY_ADDR), merges the value with the neighbouring
  entry's nibble, marks the FAT sector changed and, for an entry that straddles two sectors,
  writes both halves back. A cluster past the end is reported as error $A3 through
  DISK_ERROR, and nothing is written.
  Entry: DE = the cluster; BC = the value; HL = the drive's record; IY = EXDOS's base.
  Exit: the entry is set, its sector's buffer marked changed.
  Changes: AF, BC, all alternates.
  Keeps: DE, HL, IX, IY.
  .1194 Value $1000 or more?
  .1199 Then the end-of-chain mark $FFF

@1A48 DIR_CLEAR_SECS
  Clears A sectors of a drive, from sector DE downwards: each gets a buffer without being read
  ($0AE0 with B = 0), 512 zero bytes and the changed mark. After the first, each buffer is
  moved to the head of the chain first (BUF_TO_HEAD). When bit 2 of IY+$48 is set (a new
  directory), the lowest sector then gets the "." entry and the ".." entry, whose cluster is
  the word at IY+$30, through $18E2.
  Entry: A = the sector count; DE = the highest sector; HL = the drive's record; F: Z moves
  the first sector's buffer to the head too; IY = EXDOS's base.
  Exit: DE = the lowest sector - 1.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, all alternates.
  Keeps: HL, IX, IY.
  .1A49 IX = the drive's record, its own IX on the stack
  .1A4C B = the sector count

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Functions 14, 21 and 20

@061A FN14_FLUSH
  EXDOS function 14 (table word at $056C): writes back the changed buffers of drive B (0: the
  default drive at IY-$57; $FF: every drive). With C nonzero it then drops those buffers and
  marks every unit for a disk check ($025D).
  Entry: B = the drive number (0 default, $FF all); C = nonzero to drop the buffers too.
  Exit: A = 0, Z.
  Error: A = $AF (Invalid drive), NZ.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, IX, all alternates; page 1.
  Keeps: IY.
  .061B Drive $FF?
  .061E Drive 0?
  .0621 Then the default drive

@0624 FN14_UNIT
  Looks drive A up ($02D1); a drive without a unit is error $AF (Invalid drive).
  .0624 A = the drive's unit, 0 for none
  .0628 Unit found?
  .062A Error $AF: Invalid drive

@062D FN14_DO
  Writes back the changed buffers of unit A ($FF: all, $0BAE), then, when C is nonzero, drops
  them ($0BBE) and has every unit's disk checked again ($025D).
  .062D B = the unit, $FF for all
  .062E Write back the unit's changed buffers
  .0631 C = 0: done?
  .0634 Drop the unit's buffers
  .0637 Recheck every unit's disk

@063A FN21_FREE_SPACE
  EXDOS function 21 (table word at $057A): the free space of drive B. Reads every FAT entry from
  cluster 2 to the last one and counts the zero ones.
  Entry: B = the drive number; IY = EXDOS's base.
  Exit: A = 0, Z; HL = the free clusters; DE = the clusters in all; B = 1 + the byte at
  record+$0A; C = the record's segment; IX = record+8.
  Error: A = an error code from the drive select ($AF Invalid drive), NZ.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, IX, all alternates; page 1.
  Keeps: IY.
  .063A C = the drive
  .063B HL = the drive's record
  .063E Selected?
  .0640 IX = the record
  .0643 DE = record+$16: the cluster limit (count + 2)
  .0649 Kept on the stack for the loop's test
  .064A Start at cluster 2
  .064D BC = 0: the free count (D = 0)

@064F FN21_COUNT
  Reads the FAT entry of cluster DE and counts it in BC when it is 0.
  .0650 DE = FAT[DE]
  .0653 Free cluster?
  .0657 Count it

@0658 FN21_NEXT
  Steps to the next cluster until the limit, then builds the results.
  .0659 Next cluster
  .065A HL = the limit, the record on the stack
  .065B Limit reached?
  .065E HL = the record again
  .0661 HL = the free clusters
  .0663 DE = the limit
  .0664 DE = the clusters in all (limit - 2)
  .0669 IX = record+8
  .066B C = the segment in page 1, the record's
  .066E B = 1 + the byte at record+$0A

@0674 FN20_DRIVE_MAP
  EXDOS function 20 (table word at $0578): a map of the drives that exist. For drives 26 (Z) down
  to 1 (A) it shifts a 1 into DE:HL when the drive has a unit with a segment, so drive A ends
  in bit 0 and Z in bit 25.
  Entry: A = 0 (as $0505 calls it); IY = EXDOS's base.
  Exit: A = 0, Z; DE:HL = the drive map.
  Changes: AF, B, DE, HL, HL'.
  Keeps: C, IX, IY.
  .0674 DE:HL = 0
  .0678 Drives 26 down to 1

@067A FN20_LOOP
  Shifts in the bit of drive B.
  .067B The lookup's HL and DE are the alternates
  .067C HL = the drive's unit entry
  .067F A = its segment
  .0680 Carry when the segment is nonzero
  .0683 Shift the bit into DE:HL

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The two devices: DISK (entry table $068D) and the drive-letter device (table $06A7)

@09AB DEV_IY_BASE
  Moves IY from the device's own pointer to EXDOS's base: IY += the word at IY-5. Every device
  entry starts with it.
  Entry: IY = the pointer the device was entered with.
  Exit: IY = EXDOS's base.
  Changes: F, IY, DE'.
  Keeps: A, BC, DE, HL, IX.
  .09AB Work in the alternate set
  .09AC DE' = the word at IY-5

@06C3 DSK_IRQ
  DISK's interrupt entry (table word at $068D). Counts down every unit's disk-change timer (the
  byte at record+9) by one, down to 2, then counts down the byte at IY-$5F; when that reaches
  0 it goes on at $1C9F with B = 0.
  Entry: IY = the device's pointer.
  Exit: A = the IY-$5F count.
  Changes: AF, B, DE, HL, IY, DE'; page 1.
  Keeps: C, IX.
  .06C6 HL = IY+$0252: 26 unit entries, 3 bytes each
  .06CD 26 units

@06CF DSK_IRQ_LOOP
  Takes the next unit entry: segment, then the record's address.
  .06D1 No unit here?
  .06D4 Page the unit's segment into page 1
  .06D6 DE = the record
  .06D9 Back to the entry's address byte
  .06DA DE = record+9, the disk-change timer

@06E1 DSK_IRQ_TIMER
  Counts the unit's timer down by one while it is 3 or more.
  .06E2 Carry when below 3
  .06E4 Minus 1 only when 3 or more

@06E7 DSK_IRQ_NEXT
  Next unit; after the last, counts down IY-$5F.
  .06E7 Next entry
  .06EB A = the count at IY-$5F
  .06EE Already 0?
  .06F4 Not 0 yet?
  .06F5 B = 0

@06F9 DSK_SPECIAL
  The special function entry of both devices (table words at $06A3 and $06BD). B selects:
  $10 writes the channel's buffer and finishes its file (DSK_SPEC_FLUSH); $F0 is EXOS 24
  (allocate segment) and $F1 EXOS 25 (free segment), passed on; any other code is error $EA.
  Entry: A = the channel number; B = the function code; IX = the channel's RAM; IY = the
  device's pointer; C, DE for EXOS 24/25.
  Exit: A = 0, Z, or EXOS 24/25's results.
  Error: NZ, A = $EA for an unknown code, or the error of the work done.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, IY, all alternates; page 1.
  Keeps: IX.
  .06FC Keep the channel number in A'
  .06FE Code $10?
  .0702 Code $F0?
  .0706 Code $F1?
  .0707 Any other: error $EA
  .070A EXOS 25 (free segment), the caller's registers as they are

@070D DSK_SPEC_ALLOC
  Special function $F0: EXOS 24 (allocate segment) with the caller's registers.

@0710 DSK_SPEC_FLUSH
  Special function $10: makes the channel current, then writes its buffer and runs function $0D
  (CHAN_COMMIT).
  .0710 A = the channel number again
  .0711 Make it the current channel

@095E CHAN_SELECT
  Makes channel A the current one. The current channel's 48-byte control block is worked on at
  IY+$0724; IY+$0720 holds that channel's number (0: none), IY+$0721 the segment and IY+$0722
  the address of the channel's own copy (at IX-$34 in its RAM). For a new channel the old
  one's block is copied back into its RAM (its segment paged into page 1 for the copy) and the
  new one's copied in. Then the copy's place is noted again, which also serves after the
  channel's RAM has moved. Works in the alternate set, so BC, DE and HL are kept.
  Entry: A = the channel number; IX = its RAM; IY = EXDOS's base.
  Exit: A = 0, Z when the channel's open was completed (the byte at IX-1 is $FF).
  Error: A = $E4, NZ, for a channel whose open did not complete.
  Changes: AF, BC', DE', HL'.
  Keeps: BC, DE, HL, IX, IY.
  .0962 DE' = IX-$34: the channel's copy of its block
  .096A HL' = IY+$0720: the current channel's number
  .096E Note the new channel
  .0972 Already the current one?
  .0976 48 bytes
  .0979 No channel was current?
  .097C Keep page 1's segment
  .097F Page in the old channel's segment
  .0984 DE = the old channel's copy
  .0989 Copy the block back from IY+$0724
  .098E Page 1 back

@0992 CHSEL_LOAD
  Copies the new channel's block from its RAM to IY+$0724.
  .0992 HL = IY+$0724
  .0997 Copy the channel's block in

@099B CHSEL_NOTE
  Notes where the current channel's copy lives (segment, address), then tests the open mark.
  .099B The segment in page 1: the channel RAM's
  .099F And the copy's address
  .09A3 The open mark: $FF once the open completed
  .09A7 Open completed?
  .09A8 Error $E4

@084B CHAN_FLUSH_WR
  Writes the bytes waiting in the channel's buffer to its file: the count at IX-4 (0: none) goes
  to function $10 with the buffer at IX-$0134 and the control block at IY+$0724.
  Entry: IX = the channel's RAM; IY = EXDOS's base.
  Exit: A = 0, Z (also when nothing was waiting).
  Error: NZ, A = the error of function $10.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, IY.
  Keeps: IX.
  .084B A = the bytes waiting
  .084E Nothing to write?
  .0850 BC = the count

@0853 CHAN_WRITE_BUF
  Writes BC bytes from the channel's buffer: DE = the control block, the waiting count cleared,
  then function $10 with IX = the buffer (the channel's IX kept on the stack).
  .0857 No bytes waiting from now on
  .085B HL = the channel's buffer
  .085F IX = the buffer, the channel's IX on the stack
  .0862 Function $10: write bytes

@0803 FCB_ADDR
  DE = IY+$0724: the current channel's control block.
  Entry: IY = EXDOS's base.
  Exit: DE = IY+$0724.
  Changes: F, DE.
  Keeps: A, BC, HL, IX, IY.

@0714 CHAN_COMMIT
  When Z: writes the channel's waiting bytes (CHAN_FLUSH_WR); then runs function $0D on the
  current control block (the call close makes).
  Entry: F: Z to go on (NZ returns at once); IX = the channel's RAM; IY = EXDOS's base.
  Exit: A = 0, Z.
  Error: NZ, A = the error code.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, IY.
  Keeps: IX.
  .0718 DE = the control block
  .071B Function $0D

@071D EXDOS_FN_IX
  Runs EXDOS function A through the entry at $0505 and keeps IX.
  Entry: A = the function number; the function's own registers; IY = EXDOS's base.
  Exit: the function's results: A = 0, Z on success.
  Error: NZ, A = the error code.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, IY.
  Keeps: IX.

@071F EXDOS_FN_POPIX
  Runs EXDOS function A, then takes IX back from the stack (pushed by the caller).
  .0722 IX back

@0725 DRV_INIT
  The drive-letter device's initialise entry (table word at $06BF): stores IX at IY+$6B.
  Entry: IX, IY = the device's pointer.
  Exit: IY = EXDOS's base; HL = IX.
  Changes: F, HL, IY, DE'.
  Keeps: A, BC, DE, IX.

@072A DSK_INIT
  DISK's initialise entry (table word at $06A5): no channel is current any more.
  Entry: IY = the device's pointer.
  Exit: IY = EXDOS's base.
  Changes: F, BC, HL, IY, DE'.
  Keeps: A, DE, IX.

@095B CHAN_SELECT_IY
  CHAN_SELECT for a device entry: IY to EXDOS's base first. Also the buffer-moved entry of both
  devices (table words at $06A7 and $06C1): noting the channel's place again is all a move
  needs. ($06A7 is the drive-letter device's interrupt word too, which a device without
  interrupts never has called.)
  Entry: A = the channel number; IX = its RAM; IY = the device's pointer.
  Exit: A = 0, Z when the channel's open was completed.
  Error: A = $E4, NZ, for a channel whose open did not complete.
  Changes: AF, IY, BC', DE', HL'.
  Keeps: BC, DE, HL, IX.

@072F DSK_CLOSE
  The close and destroy entry of both devices (table words at $0693, $0695, $06AD and $06AF):
  writes the channel's waiting bytes, runs function $0D on its file and leaves no channel
  current.
  Entry: A = the channel number; IX = its RAM; IY = the device's pointer.
  Exit: A = 0, Z.
  Error: NZ, A = the error code ($E4 for a channel whose open did not complete).
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, IY, BC', DE', HL'.
  Keeps: IX.

@0735 CHAN_NONE_CUR
  IY+$0720 = 0: no channel's block is at IY+$0724 any more.

@073F DSK_OPEN
  DISK's open entry (table word at $068F); create enters one byte on (table word at $0691, the
  $B7 inside LD H,$B7: OR A), so carry is 1 for open, 0 for create. When bit 0 of IY-$37 is
  set, it only allocates (word at IY+$6B) + 1 bytes of channel RAM and notes it (IY+$6B = its
  pointer - 1, IY+$6D = the segment in page 1). Otherwise OPEN_CHAN_FILE.
  Entry: A = the channel number; DE = the counted file name; C = the drive number; IX; IY =
  the device's pointer.
  Exit: A = 0, Z.
  Error: NZ, A = the error code.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, IX, IY, all alternates; page 1.
  .073F Open: carry 1
  .0740 Hides OR A at $0741: create, carry 0
  .0742 A', F' = the channel number and open/create
  .0746 Allocate-only mode?
  .074C DE = the word at IY+$6B
  .0750 EXOS 27: allocate channel RAM
  .0755 The segment in page 1: the RAM's

@075B DEV_SAVE_IX
  IY+$6B = IX.
  .075B HL = IX

@0765 DRV_OPEN
  The drive-letter device's open entry (table word at $06A9); create enters one byte on ($06AB,
  the $B7 of LD H,$B7: OR A). The drive comes from the device's name: C = the letter at IY+8
  - $40. With bit 0 of IY-$37 set it does nothing (A = 0); otherwise OPEN_CHAN_FILE.
  Entry: A = the channel number; DE = the counted file name; IX; IY = the device's pointer.
  Exit: A = 0, Z, or A = 0 and NZ when bit 0 of IY-$37 is set.
  Error: NZ, A = the error code.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, IY, all alternates; page 1.
  Keeps: IX.
  .0765 Open: carry 1
  .0766 Hides OR A at $0767: create, carry 0
  .0768 A', F' = the channel number and open/create
  .0769 The device name's letter
  .076C C = the drive number (A = 1)
  .0773 Allocate-only mode: nothing to open?

@0778 OPEN_CHAN_FILE
  Opens or creates the file named by DE on channel A, drive C.
  1. EXOS 27 allocates $0134 bytes of channel RAM: the 256-byte buffer at IX-$0134, the
     control block's copy at IX-$34, the counts at IX-4..IX-2 and the open mark at IX-1.
  2. The channel is made current and the name's end is marked with a 0.
  3. Function 1 takes the path apart into the block at IY+$06A1 (drive at IY+$06A8); a
     nonzero C back is error $A8 (Invalid pathname string).
  4. Create runs function 6; open runs function 4, after putting "START" (or "EXDOS.INI"
     when bit 4 of IY-$37 is set) into the block when B comes back with bits 3 and 4 clear.
  5. An open file whose attribute byte (control block +$13) has bit 2 set is error $A1
     (Invalid file attributes), unless bit 5 of IY-$37 is set.
  6. Function $0C, then the open mark becomes $FF.
  .0779 The size of the channel RAM
  .077C EXOS 27: allocate channel RAM
  .077F A = the channel number from A'
  .0784 Make the new channel current
  .0787 HL = the counted name
  .078B HL = the name's first character
  .078D HL = the name's end
  .078E End the name with a 0
  .078F Default-drive mode?
  .0795 Then the drive at IY-$56

@0798 OPEN_PATH
  Sets up the path block and runs function 1 on the name.
  .079B HL = IY+$06A8
  .079F The drive
  .07A2 IY+$06A7 = 0
  .07A7 IY+$06A1 = $FF
  .07A9 DE = IY+$06A1, the path block
  .07AA HL = the name's first character
  .07AC Function 1: take the path apart
  .07B3 Failed?
  .07B4 C nonzero?
  .07B5 Then error $A8: Invalid pathname string
  .07BA A = the channel, F: carry for open
  .07BB Function 6: create
  .07BD Create?
  .07BF Bits 3 and 4 of B both clear?
  .07C4 EXDOS.INI wanted?
  .07C8 "EXDOS   INI"
  .07CD "START"

@07D0 OPEN_DEF_NAME
  Copies the 11-byte default name to the path block +8.
  .07D0 DE = the path block +8

@07DA OPEN_FIND
  A = 4: function 4, the open.

@07DC OPEN_RUN_FN
  Runs function A (4 or 6) with the control block, the path block and the name, then checks the
  attribute.
  .07DC DE = the control block
  .07DF BC = the control block too
  .07E1 DE = the path block
  .07E2 HL = the name
  .07E3 Function 4 (open) or 6 (create)
  .07E6 Failed?
  .07E7 Attributes left unchecked?
  .07ED HL = the control block +$13: the attribute byte
  .07F1 Attribute bit 2 set?
  .07F3 Then error $A1: Invalid file attributes

@07F6 OPEN_FINISH
  Function $0C, then marks the channel open.
  .07F6 HL = the path block
  .07F7 DE = the control block
  .07F9 Function $0C
  .07FE Failed?
  .07FF Open mark: $FF

@080D CHBUF_START
  HL = IX-$0134: the start of the channel's 256-byte buffer.
  Entry: IX = the channel's RAM.
  Exit: HL = the buffer; BC = $FECC.
  Changes: F, BC, HL.
  Keeps: A, DE, IX, IY.

@080F CHBUF_BYTE
  HL = IX-$0134 + C: byte C of the channel's buffer.
  Entry: C = the index; IX = the channel's RAM.
  Exit: HL = the byte's address; BC = $FECC.
  Changes: F, BC, HL.
  Keeps: A, DE, IX, IY.

@081A FILE_POS_ADDR
  DE = IY+$074C, the current file's 4-byte position; HL = IY+$073B, its 4-byte length (both in
  the control block at IY+$0724).
  Entry: IY = EXDOS's base.
  Exit: DE = the position; HL = the length.
  Changes: F, DE, HL.
  Keeps: A, BC, IX, IY.

@0827 READ_AHEAD_DROP
  Gives back the bytes read ahead and not taken: the count at IX-2 is subtracted from the
  file's position at IY+$074C (a borrow goes into its high word) and set to 0.
  Entry: IX = the channel's RAM; IY = EXDOS's base.
  Exit: A = 0; IX-2 = 0.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL.
  Keeps: IX, IY.
  .0827 A = the bytes read ahead
  .082B None?
  .082C DE = the position
  .0830 HL = its low word
  .0834 C = the count
  .0836 None read ahead from now on
  .083A Low word minus the count
  .0840 No borrow?
  .0841 The high word
  .0846 Borrow from it

@0867 DSK_READ_CHAR
  The read character entry of both devices (table words at $0697 and $06B1). Takes the next
  byte of the channel's buffer; an empty buffer is filled first: the waiting writes go out,
  then function $0F reads up to 256 bytes into it.
  Entry: A = the channel number; IX = its RAM; IY = the device's pointer.
  Exit: A = 0, Z; B = the byte.
  Error: NZ, A = the error code; A = $E4 (with Z) when nothing was left to read.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, IY, all alternates.
  Keeps: IX.
  .0867 Keep the channel number in A'
  .0868 Bytes left in the buffer?
  .086F Make the channel current
  .0872 Write the waiting bytes first
  .0876 DE = the control block
  .0879 HL = the buffer
  .087D IX = the buffer, the channel's IX on the stack
  .087F Up to 256 bytes
  .0882 Function $0F: read bytes
  .0889 Read?
  .088B Read in part ($E4)?

@088E RDCH_FILLED
  Starts on a freshly filled buffer: BC = the bytes read.
  .088F Nothing read?
  .0890 Error $E4
  .0893 Index 0
  .0897 A = the count (0 for 256)

@0898 RDCH_NEXT
  Takes the byte at the buffer's index and counts it.
  .0898 One byte fewer left
  .089C C = the index
  .08A2 B = the byte
  .08A3 Next index

@08A8 DSK_WRITE_CHAR
  The write character entry of both devices (table words at $069B and $06B5). Gives back any
  bytes read ahead, puts B into the channel's buffer, and writes the buffer when it holds 256.
  Entry: A = the channel number; B = the byte; IX = its RAM; IY = the device's pointer.
  Exit: A = 0, Z.
  Error: NZ, A = the error code.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, IY, BC', DE', HL'.
  Keeps: IX.
  .08A8 Make the channel current
  .08AD Give back the bytes read ahead
  .08B0 C = the bytes waiting: the index
  .08B7 Store the byte
  .08B9 One more waiting
  .08BC Fewer than 256?
  .08BD Write all 256

@08C3 DSK_READ_BLOCK
  The read block entry of both devices (table words at $0699 and $06B3): DSK_BLOCK with function
  $0F (read bytes).
  Entry: A = the channel number; BC = the byte count; DE = the address; IX = its RAM; IY = the
  device's pointer.
  Exit: A = 0, Z, or $E4 when the file ended first; BC = the bytes not read; DE = the address
  after the last byte; IX = the caller's DE.
  Error: NZ, A = the error code; BC = the count, DE = the address.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, IX, IY, BC', DE', HL'.
  .08C3 Function $0F: read bytes

@08C7 DSK_WRITE_BLOCK
  The write block entry of both devices (table words at $069D and $06B7): DSK_BLOCK with
  function $10 (write bytes).
  Entry: A = the channel number; BC = the byte count; DE = the address; IX = its RAM; IY = the
  device's pointer.
  Exit: A = 0, Z; BC = the bytes not written; DE = the address after the last byte; IX = the
  caller's DE.
  Error: NZ, A = the error code; BC = the count, DE = the address.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, IX, IY, BC', DE', HL'.
  .08C7 Function $10: write bytes

@08C9 DSK_BLOCK
  Moves a block through function H ($0F read, $10 write): makes the channel current, writes the
  waiting bytes, gives back the bytes read ahead, then calls the function with IX = the
  caller's address, BC = the count and DE = the control block. Its count done becomes the count
  not done.
  .08C9 The function number on the stack
  .08CA Make the channel current
  .08CD Keep the caller's BC, DE, HL in the alternates
  .08CE Write the waiting bytes
  .08D1 Failed?
  .08D3 Give back the bytes read ahead
  .08D6 The caller's registers back
  .08D7 IX = the caller's address
  .08DA DE = the control block
  .08DD A = the function
  .08DE The address on the stack
  .08E0 Count the call at IY-$80
  .08E3 The count on the stack
  .08E4 Run the function: BC = the bytes moved
  .08E7 HL = the count asked for
  .08E8 All moved?
  .08EA Moved in part ($E4)?
  .08EE DE = the address: nothing moved

@08F1 BLOCK_DONE
  BC = the bytes not moved, DE = the address after the last, IX = the caller's address.
  .08F1 HL = the bytes not moved
  .08F3 IX = the caller's address, the function's IX on the stack
  .08F5 DE = the address after the last byte

@08F6 BLOCK_COUNT
  BC = HL.

@08F9 BLOCK_FAIL
  The waiting writes failed: drops the function number, the caller's registers back.
  .08FA The caller's BC, DE, HL

@08FC DSK_READ_STATUS
  The read status entry of both devices (table words at $069F and $06B9): C = 0 when a byte can
  be read (bytes left in the buffer, or the position below the length), $FF at the end of the
  file.
  Entry: A = the channel number; IX = its RAM; IY = the device's pointer.
  Exit: A = 0; C = 0 or $FF.
  Error: NZ, A = the error code.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, IY, all alternates.
  Keeps: IX.
  .08FC Keep the channel number in A'
  .08FD Bytes left in the buffer?
  .0901 Then readable
  .0905 Make the channel current
  .0908 Write the waiting bytes
  .090C DE = the position, HL = the length
  .090F Their high bytes
  .0915 4 bytes, high byte first

@0917 RDST_CMP_LOOP
  Compares the position with the length, high byte first.
  .0918 Bytes differ?

@091F RDST_RESULT
  C = 0 when carry (readable), else $FF (end of file).
  .091F A = 0 without touching carry
  .0922 Position below the length?
  .0923 C = $FF: end of file

@29B8 ADDR_TO_PAGE1
  Turns address DE as one of four page maps sees it into a segment and a page-1 address: A
  selects the map of four segment bytes (1: $BFFC, 2: IY-$5D, 3: IY-$36, 4 or more: IY+$2C);
  the byte for DE's page (bits 14-15) is the segment, and D is moved into $4000-$7FFF.
  Entry: A = the map (1-4); DE = the address; IY = EXDOS's base.
  Exit: A = the segment; DE = the address in page 1.
  Changes: AF, D.
  Keeps: BC, E, HL, IX, IY.
  .29BA Map 1: the four bytes at $BFFC
  .29BD Map 1?
  .29C0 Map 2: IY-$5D
  .29C3 Map 2?
  .29C6 Map 3: IY-$36
  .29C9 Map 3?
  .29CC Map 4: IY+$2C

@0925 DSK_SET_STATUS
  The set status entry of both devices (table words at $06A1 and $06BB). DE points at the
  caller's 16-byte status block (paged into page 1 through the caller's page map at $BFFC):
  with bit 0 of C set its first 4 bytes become the file's position, else the position is
  written there. Then bytes 4-7 get the file's length and 8-15 are cleared.
  Entry: A = the channel number; C = the flags; DE = the block; IX = its RAM; IY = the
  device's pointer.
  Exit: A = 0; C = 3.
  Error: NZ, A = the error code.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, IY, BC', DE', HL'; page 1.
  Keeps: IX.
  .0925 Make the channel current
  .0928 Keep the caller's BC, DE, HL in the alternates
  .0929 Write the waiting bytes
  .092D Give back the bytes read ahead
  .0930 The caller's registers back
  .0931 The caller's page map at $BFFC
  .0933 A = DE's segment, DE = its page-1 address
  .0936 Page the block in
  .0938 A = the flags
  .093A DE = the position, HL = the length
  .093D HL = the block, the length's address on the stack
  .093E Bit 0 of C set?
  .0941 No: copy the position into the block

@0942 SETST_COPY
  Copies the 4 bytes of the position one way or the other.
  .0947 Position read?
  .0949 DE = the block +4

@094A SETST_LENGTH
  Bytes 4-7 = the file's length, 8-15 = 0, C = 3.
  .094A HL = the length
  .094D Copy it into the block
  .0952 Clear 8 bytes: one, then 7 more

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The buffer chain: buffers linked from IY+$22, the next one to reuse at the head

@0A1F BUF_FIND_LINK
  Follows a chain of links (each node's first word links to the next, 0 ends it) from the link
  at HL to node BC.
  Entry: HL = the first link's address; BC = the node.
  Exit: NZ, carry 0: DE = the address of the link to node BC; HL = BC.
  Error: Z: BC is not in the chain; DE = the last link's address, HL = 0.
  Changes: AF, DE, HL.
  Keeps: BC, IX, IY.
  .0A23 HL = the next node, DE = its link's address
  .0A26 End of the chain?
  .0A27 Node BC?

@09CC BUF_TO_TAIL
  Moves buffer HL to the end of the chain at IY+$22, the end used last; a buffer whose link is
  already 0 stays.
  Entry: HL = the buffer; IY = EXDOS's base.
  Exit: HL = the buffer, now last.
  Changes: AF.
  Keeps: BC, DE, HL, IX, IY.
  .09CC Link 0: last already?
  .09D4 DE = the next buffer
  .09D8 Its link 0: it will be the last
  .09DC BC = the buffer
  .09E1 HL = IY+$22: the chain's first link
  .09E5 DE = the link to the buffer
  .09E9 DE = the next buffer
  .09EA Take the buffer out of the chain
  .09EE DE = the last link

@09F1 BUF_RELINK
  Stores BC in the link at DE, then takes the registers back.
  .09F2 The link = BC

@09F7 BUF_POP_DE
  DE back, return.

@0A2E BUF_WRITE_BACK
  Writes buffer HL back to its disk when it is in use (+2 nonzero) and changed (bit 7 of +3,
  cleared now). A buffer is an 11-byte header and 512 data bytes: +0 the link, +2 the unit
  key, +3 flags, +4 the sector, +6 the number of copies, +7 the distance between copies, +8 the
  drive record's segment, +9 its address. Every copy (a FAT has one per FAT) is written by
  driver request 5 or 6; when none could be written DISK_ERROR asks, and Z from it tries again.
  Bit 6 of +3 writes only the one sector copies x distance further on.
  Entry: HL = the buffer; IY = EXDOS's base.
  Exit: bit 2 of IY+$63 is set; page 1 as it was.
  Changes: AF, all alternates.
  Keeps: BC, DE, HL, IX, IY.
  .0A2E Bit 2 of IY+$63: a buffer was looked at
  .0A35 Unit key 0: buffer not in use?
  .0A3A Changed?
  .0A3E Not changed from now on
  .0A42 Keep page 1's segment

@0A47 BUF_WB_TRY
  Takes the sector and the copies from the header.
  .0A48 Only the one copy further on?
  .0A4B DE = the sector
  .0A4F B = the number of copies

@0A54 BUF_SKIP_COPIES
  Adds the distance between copies B times.
  .0A54 Add the distance (+7)

@0A58 BUF_SKIP_NEXT
  Next copy; at the end one sector is written.
  .0A5B Write one copy

@0A5D BUF_WRITE_SETUP
  Pages in the drive's record and points IX at the data.
  .0A5F The record's segment
  .0A60 Into page 1
  .0A63 DE = the record's address
  .0A68 The data's address in page 1
  .0A6D IX = the data
  .0A6F HL = the record
  .0A70 C = 1: no copy written yet
  .0A72 DE = the sector

@0AB0 WRITE_REQUEST
  The driver request for a write: 5 when the byte at IY-$58 is nonzero, else 6.
  Entry: IY = EXDOS's base.
  Exit: A = 5 or 6.
  Changes: AF.
  Keeps: BC, DE, HL, IX, IY.
  .0AB0 The byte at IY-$58 set?
  .0AB4 Request 5
  .0AB7 Request 6

@0C5A UNIT_CHANGED
  Asks the driver of unit HL (request 2) whether its disk was changed. B = 1 back: Z (not
  changed); B = 0: decided by the byte at IY-$54 (Z when it is $FF, carry set); any other B:
  NZ.
  Entry: HL = the drive's record; IY = EXDOS's base.
  Exit: Z: the disk is the same one; NZ: it may have changed.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, IX, all alternates.
  Keeps: IY.
  .0C5A Request 2
  .0C5F Answer 1?
  .0C61 Answer other than 0?
  .0C63 0: the byte at IY-$54 decides

@0C30 UNIT_CHECK_DISK
  Makes sure the disk in unit HL is the one its record describes, before a driver request. A
  timer (record+9) above 2 means the disk was used lately, and nothing is checked; at 2 the
  driver is asked (UNIT_CHANGED). Otherwise the disk's identity is read ($0C69) and compared
  with the record ($0C7F); while it differs, error $B3 (Wrong disk) is reported and asked again.
  Entry: HL = the drive's record; IY = EXDOS's base.
  Exit: the right disk is in the unit.
  Changes: HL, IX, all alternates.
  Keeps: AF, BC, DE, IY.
  .0C33 HL = record+9: the disk-change timer
  .0C37 Above 2: used lately?
  .0C3E At 2: ask the driver
  .0C41 Not changed?

@0C1E UNIT_REQUEST
  Runs driver request A on unit HL ($0470) after UNIT_CHECK_DISK; the code from $0C24 then
  sets the disk-change timer (record+9) to 100 and returns the driver's status.
  Entry: A = the request; HL = the drive's record; the request's registers (BC, DE, IX);
  IY = EXDOS's base.
  Exit: A = the status, Z when 0; carry 0.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, IX, all alternates.
  Keeps: IY.
  .0C21 The unit's driver

@0A73 BUF_WRITE_COPY
  Writes one copy: driver request 5 or 6 for one sector ($01FF).
  .0A75 One sector
  .0A78 A = the write request
  .0A7B Write sector DE from IX
  .0A80 Failed?
  .0A82 One more copy written

@0A83 BUF_WRITE_ERR
  Error $BF ends the copies at once.
  .0A83 Error $BF?
  .0A87 Then stop: last copy, none written

@0A8A BUF_NEXT_COPY
  Steps DE to the next copy.
  .0A8B The distance between copies (header +7)

@0A93 BUF_COPY_DONE
  After the last copy: done when one was written, else DISK_ERROR (carry set unless bit 1 of
  the flags is set).
  .0A94 Next copy
  .0A96 At least one copy written?
  .0A99 Header flag 1 present?
  .0A9F Carry for DISK_ERROR (IY+$2A = $FF)

@0ED5 DISK_ERROR
  Reports disk error A on the drive whose record is at HL and returns the answer; $0ED4 enters
  with carry set. IY+$2A = $FF with carry, else 0. A set unit flag at record+9 becomes 1.
  Error $E5 (or bit 3 of IY-$37 set) aborts at once, error $BF is retried at once; otherwise
  the handler at the word at IY-$39 asks (when the byte at IY-$3A is nonzero), else $0F3F.
  Answer 1 aborts: the drive's buffers are dropped and the call ends with error $A4 (Disk
  operation aborted) through the word at IY-$5C; it does not come back here.
  Entry: A = the error code; HL = the drive's record; F: carry, kept as IY+$2A = $FF for the
  question; IY = EXDOS's base.
  Exit: Z: try again; NZ: carry on.
  Changes: AF.
  Keeps: BC, DE, HL, IX, IY.
  .0EDB A = $FF with carry, else 0
  .0EDF HL = record+8
  .0EE3 C = the unit key
  .0EE5 The disk-change timer
  .0EE6 Already 0?
  .0EE9 Then 1

@0AA0 BUF_WRITE_FAIL
  Reports the failed write (DISK_ERROR); Z comes back to try again.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The buffer chain's head: move a buffer there (the next one to reuse)

@09F9 BUF_WB_TO_HEAD
  Writes buffer HL back when changed (BUF_WRITE_BACK), then moves it to the head of the chain.
  Entry: HL = the buffer; IY = EXDOS's base.
  Exit: HL = the buffer, now first.
  Changes: AF, all alternates.
  Keeps: BC, DE, HL, IX, IY.

@09FC BUF_TO_HEAD
  Moves buffer HL to the head of the chain at IY+$22: the first buffer to be reused.
  Entry: HL = the buffer; IY = EXDOS's base.
  Exit: HL = the buffer, now first.
  Changes: AF.
  Keeps: BC, DE, HL, IX, IY.
  .09FD DE = the word at IY+$22, the chain's head

@0A01 BUF_HEAD_MOVE
  CALL $00F2 returns here, past its byte: unlinks buffer HL and puts it first.
  .0A02 Already first?
  .0A09 The buffer is the head from now on
  .0A0F BC = the buffer's next one
  .0A13 Link it to the old head
  .0A16 HL = the old head, DE = the buffer
  .0A17 BC = the buffer
  .0A19 DE = the link that still points to it
  .0A1C BC = its next one: the link skips it

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The FAT

@0AD2 FAT_SECTOR_BUF
  Gets FAT sector DE of the drive whose record is at IX into a buffer and returns it. The
  number of FAT copies to try goes to IY+$68: the byte at record+$0E, one fewer when bit 0 of
  record+$18 is set (at least 1). The code from $0AE0 (shared, A = 1 there) looks for a buffer
  that holds the sector (the one used last, IY+$24, first), else reuses the head of the chain
  (or the one after it when the head's +2 has bit 7 set), writes that back and reads the
  sector in, trying copy after copy.
  Entry: DE = the sector; IX = the drive's record; IY = EXDOS's base.
  Exit: A = 0, Z; HL = the buffer (its data from HL+$0B).
  Changes: AF, BC, HL, all alternates.
  Keeps: DE, IX, IY.
  .0AD2 B = 1: read the sector in
  .0AD4 A = the number of FATs
  .0AD7 One copy fewer for this drive?
  .0ADD Then one fewer

@11ED FAT_ENTRY_ADDR
  Finds the 12-bit FAT entry of cluster DE: its byte offset is DE x 3 / 2, so the sector is the
  FAT's first (record+$0C) + offset / 512. An entry that straddles two sectors is copied to
  IY+$66 (bit 0 of IY+$63 set, the first sector at IY+$64) and DE points there.
  Entry: DE = the cluster; HL = the drive's record; IY = EXDOS's base.
  Exit: Z; DE = the entry's two bytes; carry = the cluster is odd (the entry is the high 12
  bits).
  Error: NZ, carry: the cluster is past the last one (record+$16 - 1).
  Changes: AF, DE, all alternates.
  Keeps: BC, HL, IX, IY.
  .11F1 IX = the record
  .11F4 No straddling entry yet
  .11F8 HL = record+$16: the cluster limit
  .11FE The last cluster
  .1200 Cluster DE past it?
  .1206 HL = DE x 3
  .1208 HL = DE x 3 / 2: the byte offset, carry = odd
  .120C The odd bit on the stack
  .120E E = offset / 512
  .1212 HL = the FAT's first sector
  .1218 DE = the sector that holds the entry
  .121A HL = its buffer
  .121D Past the buffer's header
  .1222 BC = the offset within the sector
  .1227 Its last byte ($1FF)?
  .122A A = the entry's first byte
  .122E HL = IY+$63
  .1232 The entry straddles two sectors
  .1235 Note the first sector
  .1239 And the first byte
  .123C The next sector
  .1244 A = the entry's second byte
  .1246 Next to the first one
  .1247 DE will point at the two bytes

@1148 FAT_GET_ENTRY
  Reads the FAT entry of cluster DE. A value above $0FF7 (the end-of-chain marks), and a cluster
  past the last one (after error $A3 through DISK_ERROR), come back as DE = $FFFF.
  Entry: DE = the cluster; HL = the drive's record; IY = EXDOS's base.
  Exit: DE = the entry's value or $FFFF; carry 0 for a value up to $0FF7.
  Changes: AF, DE, all alternates.
  Keeps: BC, HL, IX, IY.
  .1148 DE = the entry's bytes, carry = odd
  .114B Found?

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Questions and notes

question 07BF Does an empty name (LOAD "") reach the default-name copy, opening START?
question 073F When is bit 0 of IY-$37 set, so that opening DISK only allocates channel RAM?
question 0AB0 Which of driver requests 5 and 6 writes with a verify (IY-$58 set)?

feedback missing 073F-0767 Table words $0691 and $06AB name $0741 and $0767, the $B7 inside LD H,$B7 (create entries); no card or hidden-entry line has them
feedback wrong 0A01 Marked unreached, yet it ran: CALL $00F2 at $09FD returns past its inline byte ($0A00) into $0A01
feedback missing 095B Card lists table $06A7 as a way in; that word is the drive-letter device's interrupt slot, never called (DD_IRQFLAG 0)
feedback listing 0CBE-0D70 Cards of $0CBE, $1193, $1A48, $0C1E stop at a fall-through into another range's label; the routine's work needed the listing
feedback pattern 0710-08FC Device entries use LD H,$B7 to hide an OR A entry and EX AF,AF' to carry open/create in F'; card facts lose both
feedback wrong 0470-04AE IY and the alternates are pushed at $0472-$047A and popped at $04A4-$04AE, yet every caller's facts say IY may change; Keeps: IY was settled from that
feedback wrong 09CC-0B8E HL at $09CC and IX at $0AD2 come back (push/pop at $09D3/$09F5; ex (sp),ix at $0B37 then pop ix at $0B7C) though the facts list them as changed
